<mapreducescript scriptid="customscript_mcgi_mr_trader_cache_arch">
  <description>Trader Screen cache builder for CWP ARCH (subsidiary 9). Builds On Hand from lot balances; the other five buckets are structurally zero until ARCH sales orders and purchase orders exist in the account.</description>
  <isinactive>F</isinactive>
  <name>MCGI MR Trader Screen Cache ARCH</name>
  <notifyadmins>F</notifyadmins>
  <notifyemails>aserban@houseblend.io</notifyemails>
  <!-- Notifications go to Andrei ONLY while ARCH is being built.
       notifyowner=T sent them to the script owner, "House Blend 2"
       (credentials.ns_mcg2@houseblend.io) — a shared HouseBlend
       credentials mailbox, so the person actually building this never
       saw them. notifyadmins stays F: MGSL admins must not be paged by
       our in-progress work. -->
  <notifyowner>F</notifyowner>
  <scriptfile>[/SuiteScripts/mcgi_services/trader_screen/entry_points/mr/mcgi_mr_trader_screen_cache_arch.js]</scriptfile>
  <!-- ONE script parameter, because the code actually reads it.
       The MTL fork carries four; the other three are still deliberately absent —
       the subsidiary is a constant, there is no delta mode to threshold, and no
       lot costing yet to point at an accounting book. Declaring parameters the
       code never reads is how a deployment ends up looking configurable when it
       is not. -->
  <scriptcustomfields>
    <scriptcustomfield scriptid="custscript_ts_arch_force_full_rebuild">
      <accesslevel>2</accesslevel>
      <applyformatting>F</applyformatting>
      <checkspelling>F</checkspelling>
      <defaultchecked>F</defaultchecked>
      <defaultselection></defaultselection>
      <defaultvalue></defaultvalue>
      <description>Bypasses the shrink guard, and ONLY the shrink guard. It does not make a rebuild happen sooner: the pacing gate is deliberately not bypassable, so the forced rebuild lands at the next gate opening, within one interval. Check this only when the summary is legitimately much smaller than what is cached, for example after test data is deleted. Then wait one gate opening and UNCHECK it. Leave it checked and every rebuild runs with the guard disarmed, so a broken run can silently truncate the cache.</description>
      <displayheight></displayheight>
      <displaytype>NORMAL</displaytype>
      <displaywidth></displaywidth>
      <dynamicdefault></dynamicdefault>
      <fieldtype>CHECKBOX</fieldtype>
      <help>The shrink guard refuses to replace the cached summary when the incoming row count is under half the cached count, because that pattern means a partial or errored run rather than a real change. If the shrink is genuine the guard would otherwise block it forever, so check this, run once, then UNCHECK it.</help>
      <isformula>F</isformula>
      <ismandatory>F</ismandatory>
      <label>Force Full Rebuild (bypass shrink guard)</label>
      <linktext></linktext>
      <maxlength></maxlength>
      <maxvalue></maxvalue>
      <minvalue></minvalue>
      <onparentdelete></onparentdelete>
      <searchlevel>2</searchlevel>
      <selectrecordtype></selectrecordtype>
      <setting></setting>
      <storevalue>T</storevalue>
    </scriptcustomfield>
    <scriptcustomfield scriptid="custscript_ts_arch_cost_book">
      <accesslevel>2</accesslevel>
      <applyformatting>F</applyformatting>
      <checkspelling>F</checkspelling>
      <defaultchecked>F</defaultchecked>
      <defaultselection></defaultselection>
      <defaultvalue>1</defaultvalue>
      <description>Accounting book the AVG COST column is priced in. 1 = Primary, which on a CAD subsidiary IS the CAD cost — this is what the client asked for twice (Marc-Antoine and Lucas, both "comme IND, en CAD"). Change it only if that decision is reversed; the code needs no edit.</description>
      <displayheight></displayheight>
      <displaytype>NORMAL</displaytype>
      <displaywidth></displaywidth>
      <dynamicdefault></dynamicdefault>
      <fieldtype>INTEGER</fieldtype>
      <help>MTL prices its lots in a USD SECONDARY book, whose internal id differs per environment (sandbox and production disagree). The PRIMARY book is 1 in every environment, so leaving this at 1 is environment-independent. Note that a CAD cost against a USD sales order makes the margin compare two currencies — that conversion rule is still an open question with the client and this parameter does not answer it.</help>
      <isformula>F</isformula>
      <ismandatory>F</ismandatory>
      <label>Costing Accounting Book</label>
      <linktext></linktext>
      <maxlength></maxlength>
      <maxvalue></maxvalue>
      <minvalue></minvalue>
      <onparentdelete></onparentdelete>
      <searchlevel>2</searchlevel>
      <selectrecordtype></selectrecordtype>
      <setting></setting>
      <storevalue>T</storevalue>
    </scriptcustomfield>
  </scriptcustomfields>
  <scriptdeployments>
    <scriptdeployment scriptid="customdeploy_mcgi_mr_trader_cache_arch">
      <buffersize>1</buffersize>
      <concurrencylimit>1</concurrencylimit>
      <!-- Left OFF at the deployment level so the guard is armed by default.
           It is a one-run switch, not a setting. -->
      <custscript_ts_arch_force_full_rebuild>F</custscript_ts_arch_force_full_rebuild>
      <!-- Primary book = CAD on a CAD subsidiary. See the field help. -->
      <custscript_ts_arch_cost_book>1</custscript_ts_arch_cost_book>
      <isdeployed>T</isdeployed>
      <loglevel>DEBUG</loglevel>
      <queueallstagesatonce>T</queueallstagesatonce>
      <runasrole>ADMINISTRATOR</runasrole>
      <!-- NOTSCHEDULED, deliberately, 2026-08-26. The builder reschedules ITSELF.
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           ⚠️ DO NOT SET THIS BACK TO SCHEDULED. Read this whole comment first;
           it was SCHEDULED hourly from 2026-08-18 and that is what failed.

           ── What the schedule actually did ─────────────────────────────────
           NetSuite silently stopped firing the recurrence, twice, while this
           record continued to read status=SCHEDULED throughout:

             2026-08-21 11:45 → 2026-08-25 13:32   dead, 3.5 days, screen on
                                                   fixtures while the client was
                                                   being asked to test it
             2026-08-25 13:32                      manual Save & Execute restored
                                                   the data; the recurrence did
                                                   NOT resume, no run ever
                                                   followed, and 23h later the
                                                   cache was CACHE_MISS again

           Cause never established, and it cannot be established remotely:
           startdate, enddate, starttime and recurrence are all NOT_EXPOSED to
           SuiteQL. UI only. Worse, nothing inside NetSuite can report the
           fault, because notify-on-error needs the script to RUN and error, and
           a script that is not running cannot report its own absence.

           ── Why self-rescheduling instead ─────────────────────────────────
           IND and MTL are both NOTSCHEDULED and neither has ever had this
           outage, because neither depends on the scheduler: each one's
           summarize resubmits itself to its own deployment, so the script is
           its own scheduler. ARCH now does the same, via N/task in summarize's
           `finally` block. The pacing gate in getInputData holds real rebuilds
           to roughly hourly, so the cadence is unchanged; only the trigger is.

           ⚠️ NEVER ADD A SCHEDULE BACK AS A SAFETY NET. It is not one. Every
           run resubmits exactly once, so a scheduled fire lands as an EXTRA
           chain member on top of the circulating one, and that member then
           resubmits forever too. Hourly would mean ~25 concurrent members after
           a day. concurrencylimit 1 queues them rather than preventing it. The
           safety net is external: cachecheck.mjs beside sql.mjs.

           🔴 SAVE & EXECUTE IS A PRECONDITIONED ACTION, NOT A FREE RETRY. Run
           `node cachecheck.mjs` first, every time. FRESH means the chain is alive
           and you must not press it; STALE or MISSING means it is dead and you
           press it exactly once.

           Pressing it on a live chain adds a SECOND circulating member, because
           every run resubmits itself exactly once and nothing merges or expires
           the extras. It is also undetectable: rebuild frequency stays hourly
           whatever the member count, since they share one pacing key, and the
           paced path logs nothing. `scheduledscriptinstance` accepts COUNT(*) but
           exposes no columns to SuiteQL, so no script can count them either. The
           only view is the Map/Reduce status page in the UI.

           So chain DEATH is loud, external and one action to fix, while chain
           MULTIPLICATION is silent and permanent. Prevention is the defence.

           ── The IND trap, and why it still does not apply ──────────────────
           The standing rule is "never enable the trader-cache MR's scheduled
           deployment". It is about the IND builder and it exists for three
           specific mechanisms. On 2026-08-18 all three were verified ABSENT
           here. TWO OF THEM ARE NO LONGER ABSENT as of 2026-08-26, so the
           reasoning is restated honestly rather than left to rot:

             1. IND throttles on TS_LAST_RUN_TIMESTAMP with a non-atomic
                read-then-write, so two runners both decide "not throttled" and
                both start a FULL rebuild.
                ⚠️ CHANGED: ARCH now HAS throttle state, TS_ARCH_PACE_LAST_START,
                read and written the same non-atomic way. The race needs TWO
                concurrent runners, and this deployment has one trigger, one
                chain member and concurrencylimit 1. Even if two ever did race,
                the cost here is a duplicate rebuild writing identical bytes,
                because of mechanism 2 below. For IND the same race corrupts a
                merge. Same shape, different consequence.
             2. IND's summarize READS TS_SUMMARY, merges, and writes back, so
                interleaved runs mean last-writer-wins on a partial merge and
                one run's delta silently vanishes. This summarize never reads
                SUMMARY back — it writes a complete payload built from the
                output iterator, so a concurrent run can only write the same
                bytes. STILL ABSENT, and it is what makes mechanism 1 benign.
             3. IND's summarize self-reschedules onto
                runtime.getCurrentScript().deploymentId, so enabling a second
                deployment forks an immortal chain.
                ⚠️ CHANGED: ARCH now does exactly this, on purpose. Note what
                actually forks a chain: not self-rescheduling, but a SECOND
                TRIGGER. Each run resubmits exactly once, so one trigger yields
                one circulating member forever. A second deployment, or a
                recurrence re-enabled alongside this, adds a member per fire and
                the count only ever grows.

           So ARCH is an idempotent FULL rebuild on a SINGLE deployment with
           concurrencylimit 1 and exactly one trigger, and that is the invariant
           the whole design rests on.

           THREE RULES IF YOU CHANGE THIS:
             - Never add a second deployment to this script.
             - Never re-enable a recurrence on this one. Both of these break the
               one-trigger invariant, which is now what keeps mechanism 3
               inapplicable. Where status used to be the safety control, the
               trigger COUNT is.
             - Keep TTL_SUMMARY/TTL_DETAIL well above the rebuild interval.
               They are 12h against a 1h interval, which is far more margin than
               the old rule required, and deliberately so: the failure mode is no
               longer a nightly gap but chain death, and the TTL is now the only
               thing between that and an empty screen. Full reasoning is in
               cacheKeys_arch.js. Do not trim it toward the interval.

           HISTORICAL, kept because it was expensive to learn and would be
           needed again if anyone ever re-adds a recurrence: `repeat` is an ISO
           8601 duration, PT1H, not a word enum, and that value came from what
           SDF actually wrote after the change was made in the UI rather than
           from a guess. `starttime` is UTC, so 11:00Z was 07:00 PT. Measured
           behaviour of that schedule while it did work: it repeated hourly from
           the 11:00Z start for ~16h then stopped until the next day, leaving a
           ~8h nightly gap. It was never continuous. -->
      <status>NOTSCHEDULED</status>
      <title>MCGI MR Trader Screen Cache ARCH</title>
      <yieldaftermins>60</yieldaftermins>
    </scriptdeployment>
  </scriptdeployments>
</mapreducescript>
